  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A dead pope was propped on a throne and questioned. He had been buried nine months. Rome dressed the body in papal vestments anyway.",
        "visual_query": "roman basilica stone interior",
        "visual_fallback": "candlelit stone hall"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Running the trial was the next pope, Stephen the Sixth \u2014 prosecutor and judge at once. A deacon crouched behind the corpse and spoke its answers.",
        "visual_query": "medieval italian cathedral nave",
        "visual_fallback": "cathedral columns"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The charges were perjury and holding office illegally. Every one aimed at erasing Formosus from the record. Who was this trial meant to convince?",
        "visual_query": "ancient roman latin manuscript",
        "visual_fallback": "old latin script"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Guilty. The papacy voided, the vestments stripped, three fingers severed, the body thrown into the Tiber. But Rome had watched all of it.",
        "visual_query": "tiber river rome bridges",
        "visual_fallback": "river at dusk"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Within months a rising deposed Stephen and he died in prison. A new pope annulled the trial, reburied Formosus in St Peter's, and banned corpse trials.",
        "visual_query": "saint peters basilica rome",
        "visual_fallback": "basilica dome"
      }
    ],
